QB.add(8,[{"id":8001,"t":"Learning","s":"Which definition of learning is used (Nolen-Hoeksema, 2014)?","o":["A relatively permanent change in behaviour resulting from experience","A relatively permanent change in knowledge, whether or not behaviour changes","A temporary change in behaviour resulting from experience","A relatively permanent change in behaviour resulting from maturation","A relatively permanent change in behaviour caused by reflexes"],"e":"Learning is <b>‘a relatively permanent change in behaviour that occurs as a result of experience’</b> (Nolen-Hoeksema, 2014: p. 225). The lecturer stressed the importance of <b>behaviour</b>: we show we have learnt something by showing our behaviour has changed because of some experience. This is more specific than the everyday meaning of “learning”.","w":["The definition stresses behaviour.","Relatively permanent.","It results from experience.","Reflexes are not learnt."],"k":"Learning = relatively permanent behaviour change from experience.","rn":[["","Learning","Learning","Relatively permanent change; In behaviour; As a result of experience"],["","How do psychologists define learning?","How do psychologists define learning?","Relatively permanent change in behaviour; As a result of experience"]]},{"id":8002,"t":"Learning","s":"How does associative learning differ from deliberately reading a book about swimming?","o":["Associative learning is less intentional (Pavlov’s dogs)","Associative learning is more intentional than reading a book","Associative learning involves no change in behaviour at all","Reading about swimming is an example of classical conditioning","Associative learning only happens when a reward is given"],"e":"Reading a swimming book is an <b>intentional</b> way of learning. <b>Associative learning</b> is a <b>less intentional</b> type: Pavlov's dogs are not salivating intentionally; they associate a response with a stimulus. Associative learning includes classical and operant conditioning.","w":["Less intentional.","Behaviour changes.","Reading is intentional learning.","Classical conditioning has no reward."],"k":"Associative learning is less intentional.","rn":[["","Why are Pavlov's dogs an example of less intentional learning?","Why are Pavlov's dogs an example of less intentional learning?","Associate response with stimulus; Not done on purpose"],["","Associative learning vs reading a book: intention","Associative learning vs reading a book: intention","Associative: less intentional; Reading a book: intentional"],["","Is associative learning more or less intentional?","Is associative learning more or less intentional?","Less intentional"]]},{"id":8003,"t":"Learning","s":"What are the two main reasons given for clinicians to learn about learning?","o":["Unhealthy behaviours are often learnt; clinicians can help people learn healthy ones","Unhealthy behaviours are mostly innate; clinicians can help people suppress them","Unhealthy behaviours are often learnt; only specialists can help people unlearn them","Learning theory is needed to prescribe and is assessed in exams","Patients rarely change, so clinicians must change their own behaviour"],"e":"The slide: <b>unhealthy behaviours are often learnt behaviours</b>, and <b>clinicians can help others learn healthy behaviours</b>, so it helps to know about learning processes. If behaviours are learnt, we can work out how people might unlearn them and learn healthy ones.","w":["Often learnt.","Clinicians can help.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Unhealthy behaviours are learnt; healthy ones can be learnt.","rn":[["","Why should clinicians learn about learning?","Why should clinicians learn about learning?","Unhealthy behaviours are learnt; Help others learn healthy behaviours"],["","If unhealthy behaviours are learnt, what can patients also do?","If unhealthy behaviours are learnt, what can patients also do?","Unlearn them; Learn healthy ones instead"]]},{"id":8004,"t":"Learning","s":"An autistic patient finds one-to-one interaction difficult. To support a capacitous decision, the doctor invites a family member into the room. Why does this help?","o":["Knowing how people learn helps patients understand, retain and communicate","It is classical conditioning, pairing the family with the doctor","It is negative reinforcement, removing the stress of one-to-one talk","It shows capacity is fixed and cannot be supported by clinicians","It is observational learning from the family member’s behaviour"],"e":"To have capacity a patient must be able to <b>understand, retain and communicate</b> information. The Mental Capacity Act (code of practice) requires clinicians to try everything to let a patient make a capacitous decision, which needs knowledge of how people learn – e.g. an <b>autistic</b> person might learn and communicate better with a <b>family member</b> present.","w":["Not conditioning.","Not the lecture’s point.","Capacity can be supported.","Not the point."],"k":"Learning theory supports capacity assessment.","rn":[["","Which 3 things must a patient do to have capacity?","Which 3 things must a patient do to have capacity?","Understand the information; Retain it; Communicate it back"]]},{"id":8005,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A patient with type 2 diabetes keeps eating sweets late at night because it “calms her down” after stressful shifts. Which mechanism most likely maintains this habit?","o":["Negative reinforcement: eating removes an unpleasant state","Positive punishment: eating adds guilt, so she eats more","Classical conditioning: sweets have become a neutral stimulus","Negative punishment: eating removes her stress, so she eats less","Observational learning only"],"e":"Negative reinforcement = taking away something bad, making the behaviour more likely. Like smoking to relieve withdrawal, eating to relieve stress is maintained because it removes an unpleasant state.","w":["Punishment makes a behaviour LESS likely.","A neutral stimulus causes no response; the sweets are a consequence of behaviour.","Removing something bad and making the behaviour more likely is negative reinforcement, not punishment.","Nothing in the vignette shows a role model."],"k":"Relief of a bad state → negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"]]},{"id":8006,"t":"Learning","s":"Classical and operant conditioning are both forms of which type of learning?","e":"Associative learning includes classical and instrumental (operant) conditioning; it is less intentional than reading a book.","k":"Associative learning.","a":["associative learning","associative"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 types of associative learning?","What are the 2 types of associative learning?","Classical conditioning; Instrumental (operant) conditioning"],["","Associative learning vs reading a book: intention","Associative learning vs reading a book: intention","Associative: less intentional; Reading a book: intentional"],["","What do classical and operant conditioning have in common?","What do classical and operant conditioning have in common?","Both are associative learning; Both less intentional"]]},{"id":8007,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Name the type of learning in which a neutral stimulus becomes associated with another stimulus through repeated pairing.","e":"Classical (Pavlovian) conditioning.","k":"Classical conditioning.","a":["classical conditioning","pavlovian conditioning","classical","pavlovian"],"rn":[["","Classical conditioning","Classical conditioning","A neutral stimulus; Becomes associated with another stimulus; Through repeated pairing"]]},{"id":8008,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A dog salivates when food is put in her bowl. After many dinners she salivates when the empty bowl is put down. What is the bowl after conditioning?","o":["The conditioned stimulus","The unconditioned stimulus","The conditioned response","A neutral stimulus","The unconditioned response"],"e":"Before conditioning the bowl was a <b>neutral stimulus</b>; after repeated pairing with food (the <b>unconditioned stimulus</b>) it becomes a <b>conditioned stimulus</b> that alone causes salivation (the conditioned response).","w":["Food is the US.","Salivation is the response.","Before conditioning.","Salivating to food."],"k":"After pairing, NS → CS.","rn":[["","CS","CS","Conditioned stimulus"],["","Classical conditioning","Classical conditioning","A neutral stimulus; Becomes associated with another stimulus; Through repeated pairing"],["","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned: the conditioned stimulus (light); Unconditioned: the unconditioned stimulus (food)"]]},{"id":8009,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Salivating in response to food, a reflex that needs no learning, is called what?","e":"Unconditioned response: an unlearned response, like a reflex, to the unconditioned stimulus.","k":"Unconditioned response.","a":["unconditioned response","ur","unconditioned reflex"],"rn":[["","Unconditioned Response (UR)","Unconditioned Response (UR)","Unlearned response; Like a reflex; e.g. salivating"],["","What is the unconditioned response like?","What is the unconditioned response like?","A reflex"]]},{"id":8010,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which statement about the UR and the CR is correct?","o":["Same behaviour; they differ in trigger and in being learnt","Different behaviours; they differ in trigger and in being learnt","Same behaviour; the UR is learnt and the CR is innate","Same behaviour; both are triggered by the unconditioned stimulus","Different behaviours; the CR occurs only after punishment"],"e":"The <b>UR and CR are the same behaviour</b> (salivating). The UR is unlearned and triggered by the US (food); the CR is <b>learnt</b> and triggered by the CS (light). Memory aid: the response stays the same; the stimulus changes.","w":["Same behaviour.","Reversed.","CR to CS.","No punishment."],"k":"UR and CR: same behaviour, different trigger.","rn":[["","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","By observation (vicarious learning)"],["","What may illness behaviours be?","What may illness behaviours be?","Learnt behaviours"],["","How do we show we have learnt something?","How do we show we have learnt something?","Our behaviour has changed"]]},{"id":8011,"t":"Classical Conditioning","f":"lr14","s":"What happens during the conditioning phase?","o":["The neutral stimulus is paired with the unconditioned stimulus","The conditioned stimulus is presented alone without food","The unconditioned stimulus is presented alone without the light","The neutral stimulus is paired with a reward for a voluntary act","The animal watches another animal being fed"],"e":"In the <b>conditioning phase</b> the <b>NS is paired with the US</b> – e.g. the light is switched on whenever food is present and off when there is no food – so the dog associates the light with food.","w":["That is after conditioning.","Before conditioning.","Operant.","Observational."],"k":"Conditioning phase = NS paired with US.","rn":[["","What is the pairing of the neutral and unconditioned stimulus called?","What is the pairing of the neutral and unconditioned stimulus called?","The conditioning phase"],["","What is the neutral stimulus paired with?","What is the neutral stimulus paired with?","The unconditioned stimulus"]]},{"id":8012,"t":"Classical Conditioning","f":"lr15","s":"After conditioning, food is removed and only the light is shown, yet the dog salivates. Which labels are correct?","o":["Light = CS; salivation = CR","Light = US; salivation = UR","Light = NS; salivation = UR","Light = CS; salivation = UR","Food = CS; salivation = CR"],"e":"After conditioning, the former neutral stimulus (<b>light</b>) is a <b>conditioned stimulus</b>, and the salivation it produces is the <b>conditioned response</b>.","w":["Light was neutral, now conditioned.","After conditioning.","Response to CS is CR.","Food is removed."],"k":"After: light = CS → CR.","rn":[["","After conditioning: what happens?","After conditioning: what happens?","CS (light) alone; Causes CR (salivation)"],["","Image card: Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","After conditioning; Light (CS) alone; Causes salivation (CR)"],["","Image card: Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","During conditioning; Light (CS) + food (US); Food causes salivation (UR)"]]},{"id":8013,"t":"Classical Conditioning","f":"lr13","s":"Before conditioning, food makes a dog salivate and a light does not. Which statement is correct?","o":["Food (US) gives salivation (UR); the light is a neutral stimulus","Food (CS) gives salivation (CR); the light is a neutral stimulus","Food (US) gives salivation (UR); the light is a conditioned stimulus","Light (US) gives salivation (UR); food is a neutral stimulus","Food (US) gives salivation (CR); the light is a neutral stimulus"],"e":"Before conditioning: the <b>US (food)</b> causes the <b>UR (salivating)</b>, which is not learnt. The <b>light</b> is just a <b>neutral stimulus</b>.","w":["Food is US.","Not yet conditioned.","Reversed.","Not learnt."],"k":"Before: US → UR; light = NS.","rn":[["","Image card: Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Before conditioning; US (food) causes UR (salivation); NS (light) causes no response"],["","Image card: Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","During conditioning; Light (CS) + food (US); Food causes salivation (UR)"],["","Unconditioned vs conditioned stimulus in the light example","Unconditioned vs conditioned stimulus in the light example","US: food; CS: light"]]},{"id":8014,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Children given ice cream after chemotherapy later felt sick at the sight of ice cream. What is the unconditioned stimulus?","e":"Bernstein (1991): chemotherapy (US) → nausea (UR); ice cream (CS) → aversion (CR).","k":"Chemotherapy.","a":["chemotherapy","chemo","the chemotherapy"],"rn":[["","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","Chemotherapy nausea; And ice cream; (Bernstein, 1991)"],["","In Bernstein (1991), what is the unconditioned stimulus?","In Bernstein (1991), what is the unconditioned stimulus?","Chemotherapy"]]},{"id":8015,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"In the chemotherapy–ice cream example, what is the conditioned response?","e":"Ice cream (CS) comes to trigger aversion/nausea (CR).","k":"Aversion to ice cream.","a":["aversion to ice cream","aversion","ice cream aversion","nausea at ice cream","disgust"],"rn":[["","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","Aversion to ice cream"],["","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Linked ice cream with; Chemo nausea"]]},{"id":8016,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Why is the chemotherapy–ice cream aversion described as “inadvertent” conditioning?","o":["Parents meant well, but the pairing conditioned an aversion","Doctors paired ice cream with chemo to reduce nausea","Children chose to avoid ice cream after seeing others refuse it","Parents rewarded children with ice cream for taking chemo","Children learned the aversion from the hospital staff"],"e":"Parents and caregivers wanted to do something nice, so gave ice cream after chemotherapy; because nausea followed chemo, the children came to associate ice cream with nausea – <b>inadvertent classical conditioning</b>.","w":["Unintentional.","Not observational.","Not operant.","Not stated."],"k":"Unintended pairing → aversion.","rn":[["","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","Aversion to ice cream"]]},{"id":8017,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A student suggested the placebo effect may partly be classical conditioning. Which explanation fits?","o":["People may be conditioned to feel better just by taking a pill","People feel better because placebos contain small doses of drug","People feel better because the placebo removes something bad","People feel better after watching others benefit from pills","People feel better because they are rewarded for taking pills"],"e":"In trials a drug is compared to a <b>placebo</b>, perhaps because people have been <b>conditioned to feel better just by taking a pill</b>: the pill (previously neutral) has been paired with feeling better.","w":["They contain no active drug.","Not the explanation.","Observational.","Operant."],"k":"Placebo as conditioning to pill-taking.","rn":[["","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Conditioned to feel better; Just by taking a pill"]]},{"id":8018,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"In a trial, patients given an inert tablet that looks like the drug improve. Which learning explanation fits?","o":["Classical conditioning: taking a pill has been paired with feeling better","Operant punishment: they are punished for not improving","Observational learning only","Negative reinforcement of side effects","Unconditioned response to the inert tablet"],"e":"The placebo effect may partly be classical conditioning: people have repeatedly paired taking a pill with feeling better, so the pill alone produces improvement – which is why trials compare against a placebo.","w":["No punishment is involved.","Not necessarily copying anyone.","No unpleasant state is removed by the action here.","An inert tablet cannot trigger an unlearnt reflex."],"k":"Placebo: conditioned pill response.","rn":[["","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Conditioned to feel better; Just by taking a pill"]]},{"id":8019,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A drug of abuse produces intense pleasure, and stopping it brings withdrawal. Which combination maintains its use?","e":"As with smoking: the good feeling is positive reinforcement (adding something good), and using to avoid withdrawal is negative reinforcement (taking away something bad). Both make use more likely.","k":"Positive + negative reinforcement.","a":["positive and negative reinforcement","positive reinforcement and negative reinforcement","both positive and negative reinforcement","positive & negative reinforcement"],"rn":[["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive: add something good; Negative: remove something bad"],["","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","Positive; Negative"]]},{"id":8020,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Can a classically conditioned association be lost?","o":["They can be unconditioned as well as conditioned","They can never be lost once conditioned","They are lost after one day without pairing","They are lost only if punishment is used","They can be lost by humans but not by dogs"],"e":"The lecturer thought dogs, like humans, can forget; with input from a student with A-level psychology he concluded it <b>can be unconditioned as well as conditioned</b>, imagining there are limits (“you can't teach an old dog new tricks”).","w":["They can be.","Not stated.","Not punishment.","Dogs can forget too."],"k":"Conditioning can be reversed.","rn":[["","Can a conditioned association be lost?","Can a conditioned association be lost?","Yes, it can be forgotten; Unconditioned as well as conditioned"],["","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned: the conditioned stimulus (light); Unconditioned: the unconditioned stimulus (food)"]]},{"id":8021,"t":"Learning","s":"Name the type of learning in which voluntary behaviour is modified by its consequences.","e":"Operant (instrumental) conditioning: reinforcement makes behaviour more likely, punishment less likely.","k":"Operant conditioning.","a":["operant conditioning","instrumental conditioning","operant","instrumental"],"rn":[["","In which type of conditioning do the consequences of behaviour matter?","In which type of conditioning do the consequences of behaviour matter?","Operant (instrumental) conditioning"],["","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Learning to modify voluntary behaviours; In light of consequences"],["","Operant conditioning","Operant conditioning","Instrumental conditioning"]]},{"id":8022,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Skinner rewarded a pigeon only when it turned the right way, until it performed a complete turn. Which type of learning and which component is this?","o":["Operant – positive reinforcement","Operant – negative reinforcement","Operant – positive punishment","Classical – conditioned stimulus","Observational – vicarious learning"],"e":"Skinner's pigeon was rewarded (food added) for the desired voluntary behaviour – <b>operant conditioning</b> by <b>positive reinforcement</b> (adding something good makes the behaviour more likely).","w":["Food is added.","Behaviour increased.","Voluntary behaviour.","No model."],"k":"Skinner's pigeon = positive reinforcement.","rn":[["","Giving a pigeon a treat for turning is what?","Giving a pigeon a treat for turning is what?","Positive reinforcement"],["","Positive Reinforcement","Positive Reinforcement","Adding something good"],["","What are the 4 types of operant consequence?","What are the 4 types of operant consequence?","Positive reinforcement; Negative reinforcement; Positive punishment; Negative punishment; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":8023,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"In operant conditioning, what do “positive” and “negative” refer to?","o":["Positive = adding something; negative = taking it away","Positive = a good outcome; negative = a bad outcome","Positive = reinforcement; negative = punishment","Positive = voluntary; negative = involuntary","Positive = more likely; negative = less likely"],"e":"“<b>Positive is always to do with the addition, negative is to do with taking something away.</b>” Whether it is reinforcement or punishment depends on whether behaviour becomes more or less likely.","w":["About addition/removal.","Both can be either.","Not stated.","That is reinforcement vs punishment."],"k":"Positive = add; negative = remove.","rn":[["","Negative Punishment","Negative Punishment","Taking away something good"],["","In operant conditioning, what does \"negative\" always mean?","In operant conditioning, what does \"negative\" always mean?","Taking something away"],["","Negative Reinforcement","Negative Reinforcement","Taking away something bad"]]},{"id":8024,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Pressing a lever stops an unpleasant noise, and the rat presses it more often. Name this consequence.","e":"Taking away something bad that makes behaviour more likely = negative reinforcement.","k":"Negative reinforcement.","a":["negative reinforcement"],"rn":[["","What is an example of negative reinforcement?","What is an example of negative reinforcement?","Pressing a lever; Stops an unpleasant noise"],["","Pressing a lever stops an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Pressing a lever stops an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Negative reinforcement"],["","Negative Reinforcement","Negative Reinforcement","Taking away something bad"]]},{"id":8025,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A teenager loses her phone after skipping her insulin dose and skips fewer doses. Name this consequence.","e":"Taking away something good that makes behaviour less likely = negative punishment.","k":"Negative punishment.","a":["negative punishment"],"rn":[["","Negative Punishment","Negative Punishment","Taking away something good"],["","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","No; Makes behaviour more likely"],["","Punishment","Punishment","Makes the behaviour less likely; Can be positive or negative"]]},{"id":8026,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Pressing a lever causes an unpleasant noise, so the rat presses it less. Name this consequence.","e":"Adding something bad that makes behaviour less likely = positive punishment.","k":"Positive punishment.","a":["positive punishment"],"rn":[["","What is an example of positive punishment?","What is an example of positive punishment?","Pressing the lever; Causes an unpleasant noise"],["","Pressing the lever causes an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Pressing the lever causes an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Positive punishment"],["","Positive Punishment","Positive Punishment","Adding something bad"]]},{"id":8027,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Which statement about negative reinforcement is correct?","o":["It removes something bad and makes behaviour more likely","It removes something good and makes behaviour less likely","It adds something bad and makes behaviour less likely","It removes something bad and makes behaviour less likely","It adds something good and makes behaviour more likely"],"e":"“Negative reinforcement” is <b>not</b> punishment: it <b>removes something bad</b>, so the behaviour goes <b>up</b>. A common trick question.","w":["Negative punishment.","Positive punishment.","It increases behaviour.","Positive reinforcement."],"k":"Negative reinforcement ≠ punishment.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"]]},{"id":8028,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"What two questions should you ask to classify any operant example?","o":["Added or removed? More or less likely?","Neutral stimulus? Reflex response?","Added or removed? Voluntary or involuntary?","Rewarded or punished? Similar model?","Intentional? Associative?"],"e":"First ask: is something <b>added or removed</b> (positive/negative)? Then: does the behaviour become <b>more or less likely</b> (reinforcement/punishment)?","w":["Classical questions.","Second is about likelihood.","Observational.","Not the two."],"k":"Added/removed + more/less likely.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","After conditioning, what can be removed?","After conditioning, what can be removed?","The unconditioned stimulus (food)"]]},{"id":8029,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Staff get a free fruit pot when they use the stairs, and stair use rises. Name this consequence.","e":"Adding something good that makes behaviour more likely = positive reinforcement.","k":"Positive reinforcement.","a":["positive reinforcement"],"rn":[["","Positive Reinforcement","Positive Reinforcement","Adding something good"],["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"]]},{"id":8030,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A smoker lights up to stop unpleasant withdrawal symptoms. Which type of operant conditioning is maintaining smoking?","o":["Negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Observational learning"],"e":"Smoking <b>removes</b> something bad (withdrawal), making smoking <b>more likely</b> = <b>negative reinforcement</b>. Smoking is maintained by both positive and negative reinforcement.","w":["That is the good feeling.","Behaviour increases.","Behaviour increases.","Not observation."],"k":"Withdrawal relief = negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Negative reinforcement"],["","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","Positive; Negative"]]},{"id":8031,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A practice nurse gives children a sticker after their vaccinations. What is the intended mechanism?","o":["Positive reinforcement of attending","Negative reinforcement, removing the pain of the jab","Classical conditioning, pairing the jab with a sticker","Positive punishment of crying during the jab","Observational learning from other children’s stickers"],"e":"<b>Stickers after injections</b> add something good to reinforce the behaviour – <b>positive reinforcement</b>, encouraging children <b>and their parents</b> to continue.","w":["Adds something good.","Not pairing; a reward for behaviour.","Not punishment.","Not the intended mechanism."],"k":"Stickers = positive reinforcement.","rn":[["","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Positive reinforcement; Rewards reinforce behaviour"]]},{"id":8032,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A practice introduces fines for missed appointments. How is this best classified?","o":["Punishment to make non-attendance less likely","Negative reinforcement to make attendance more likely","Positive reinforcement to make attendance more likely","Classical conditioning to link missed appointments with cost","Observational learning from patients who were fined"],"e":"Fines are a <b>punishment</b> to make missing appointments <b>less likely</b>. He described it first as <b>adding something bad</b> (positive punishment), then as <b>taking away something good</b> (negative punishment) – showing the classification depends on framing.","w":["Fines are punishment.","Not adding good.","Not pairing.","Not stated."],"k":"Fines = punishment (framed as add bad or remove good).","rn":[["","What does punishment do to a behaviour?","What does punishment do to a behaviour?","Makes it less likely"],["","Punishment","Punishment","Makes the behaviour less likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","No; Makes behaviour more likely"]]},{"id":8033,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A government introduces a sugar tax to reduce consumption of sugary foods. Which principle does this use?","o":["Operant – an unwanted consequence makes it less likely","Operant – a wanted consequence makes it more likely","Classical – pairing sugar with a negative stimulus","Observational – copying people who avoid sugar","Operant – removing something bad makes it more likely"],"e":"<b>Public health measures</b> such as <b>sugar taxes</b> make sugary foods more expensive to make eating them <b>less likely</b> – applying operant (consequence-based) principles.","w":["Reduces behaviour.","Not pairing.","Not modelling.","Negative reinforcement."],"k":"Sugar tax = consequence to reduce behaviour.","rn":[["","How do sugar taxes work as operant conditioning?","How do sugar taxes work as operant conditioning?","Make sugary food more expensive; Less likely to eat it"]]},{"id":8034,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A public-health team wants fewer fizzy drinks sold. Which policy uses operant punishment, and which uses observational learning?","o":["A sugar tax; adverts showing admired athletes choosing water","A sugar tax; free fruit for buying water","Adverts with athletes; a sugar tax","Free fruit for buying water; a sugar tax","A sugar tax; pairing drinks with a bad smell"],"e":"Making sugary drinks more expensive makes buying them less likely (punishment – an unwanted consequence). Copying a high-status, similar role model is observational learning.","w":["Free fruit is positive reinforcement of an alternative, not observational learning.","Reversed.","Free fruit is reinforcement; the tax is punishment.","Pairing with a smell is classical conditioning."],"k":"Tax = punishment; role model = observational."},{"id":8035,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"An adult fears spiders, yet very young children happily play with spiders and snakes. What does this suggest about phobias?","o":["Such fears are learnt behaviours","Fears are innate reflexes","Children have no learning capacity","Phobias cannot be treated","Fear is an unconditioned response to all animals"],"e":"Young children happily play with spiders and snakes, so these <b>fears are learnt behaviours</b>, which links to phobias and their treatment.","w":["Opposite.","Not stated.","Treatment was mentioned.","Not stated."],"k":"Phobias can be learnt.","rn":[["","Are phobias, like fear of spiders, innate or learnt?","Are phobias, like fear of spiders, innate or learnt?","Learnt behaviours; Young children play with them"]]},{"id":8036,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement best compares classical and operant conditioning?","o":["Classical: involuntary response, pairing; operant: voluntary, consequences","Classical: voluntary, consequences; operant: involuntary response, pairing","Classical: involuntary response, consequences; operant: voluntary, pairing","Both: voluntary behaviour changed by consequences","Both: involuntary responses changed by pairing"],"e":"Both are <b>associative</b>. <b>Classical</b>: an <b>involuntary</b> response becomes linked to a new <b>stimulus</b> by pairing. <b>Operant</b>: <b>voluntary</b> behaviour is made more or less likely by its <b>consequences</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Mixed.","Classical is involuntary.","Operant is consequences."],"k":"Classical = stimulus pairing (involuntary); operant = consequences (voluntary).","rn":[["","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical: stimulus with response; Operant: behaviour with consequences"],["","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical: reflex (unlearned) response; Operant: voluntary behaviour"],["","What is operant conditioning?","What is operant conditioning?","Modifying voluntary behaviours; In light of consequences"]]},{"id":8037,"t":"Learning","s":"Which pairing of learning theory and key figure is correct?","o":["Classical – Pavlov; operant – Skinner; observational – Bandura","Classical – Skinner; operant – Pavlov; observational – Bandura","Classical – Pavlov; operant – Bandura; observational – Skinner","Classical – Bandura; operant – Skinner; observational – Pavlov","Classical – Pavlov; operant – Skinner; observational – Benbassat"],"e":"Classical (Pavlovian) – <b>Pavlov</b>; operant (instrumental) – <b>Skinner</b>; observational (social learning) – <b>Bandura</b> (Bobo doll).","w":["Swapped.","Swapped.","Swapped.","Bandura (Bobo doll)."],"k":"Pavlov, Skinner, Bandura.","rn":[["","Classical vs operant conditioning: famous example","Classical vs operant conditioning: famous example","Classical: Pavlov's dogs; Operant: Skinner's pigeons"]]},{"id":8038,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"What is the name of the theory that people learn by observing and imitating others?","e":"Observational learning = social learning theory (Bandura); vicarious and enactive learning; imitating a role model.","k":"Social learning theory.","a":["social learning theory","social learning","observational learning"],"rn":[["","What is the theory of observational learning called?","What is the theory of observational learning called?","Social learning theory"],["","Which theory is observational learning part of?","Which theory is observational learning part of?","Social learning theory"],["","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Vicarious; Enactive"]]},{"id":8039,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"In the Bobo doll study (Bandura et al. 1961), what did the children do?","o":["Copied the adults’ hitting when left alone with the doll","Copied the adults’ hitting only when rewarded for it","Avoided the doll after seeing the adults punished","Hit the doll only if they had done so before","Ignored the doll and played with other toys"],"e":"Children watched <b>adult role models beating up a Bobo doll</b> (an inflatable clown that bounces back); when left alone with it, they <b>copied</b> the behaviour. It was a controlled experiment comparing with a group who did not see this.","w":["They copied without reward.","Not stated.","Not stated.","They copied."],"k":"Bobo doll: children imitated aggression.","rn":[["","What did children do alone with the Bobo doll?","What did children do alone with the Bobo doll?","Copied the adults; Beat it up"]]},{"id":8040,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"What gender difference was found in the Bobo doll study?","o":["Boys copied adult males more; girls copied adult females more","Girls copied adult males more; boys copied adult females more","Boys and girls both copied adult males more","Boys and girls copied both sexes equally","Only boys copied, and only from adult males"],"e":"Children did not copy just anyone: <b>boys</b> were more likely to copy <b>adult males</b>, <b>girls</b> the <b>adult women</b> – showing the characteristics of the model matter (e.g. similarity).","w":["Reversed.","Same-gender.","There was a difference.","Girls copied too."],"k":"Bobo: same-gender models copied more.","rn":[["","Which gender differences were found in the Bobo doll study?","Which gender differences were found in the Bobo doll study?","Boys copied adult men more; Girls copied adult women more"]]},{"id":8041,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which set lists the four requirements of observational learning?","o":["Attention, retention, production, motivation","Stimulus, response, reinforcement, punishment","Status, power, reliability, similarity","Observer, model, outcomes, context","Pairing, extinction, generalisation, discrimination"],"e":"Observational learning requires <b>attention</b> (watch), <b>retention</b> (remember), <b>production</b> (replicate) and <b>motivation</b> (have the behaviour reinforced). Memory aid: ARPM.","w":["Conditioning elements.","Role-model influence.","Factors affecting learning (three).","Not covered."],"k":"ARPM: attention, retention, production, motivation.","rn":[["","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","Attention; Retention; Production; Motivation; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":8042,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"During the pandemic, children watched The Wiggles' hand-washing song, remembered it, washed their hands and were praised. Which requirement does the praise represent?","o":["Motivation – reinforcement","Attention – watching the song","Retention – remembering the song","Production – washing the hands","Motivation – watching the song again and again"],"e":"<b>Motivation</b> = having the behaviour <b>reinforced</b>. Watching = attention; remembering via the song = retention; washing = production.","w":["Different requirement.","Different requirement.","Different requirement.","Motivation is the behaviour being reinforced."],"k":"Reinforcement = motivation.","rn":[["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"],["","Motivation","Motivation","Have the behaviour reinforced"]]},{"id":8043,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A student watches a skilled surgeon but, despite remembering every step, cannot physically perform the technique yet. Which requirement of observational learning is missing?","o":["Production","Attention","Retention","Motivation","Similarity"],"e":"<b>Production</b> = being able to <b>replicate</b> the behaviour. The lecturer noted that some behaviours he could never imitate however much he watched (the observer's ability to re-enact).","w":["They watched.","They remembered.","Not the issue described.","A role-model factor."],"k":"Production = ability to replicate.","rn":[["","Production","Production","Replicate the behaviour"]]},{"id":8044,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which three factors affect observational learning?","o":["The observer, the model and the outcomes","The observer, the stimulus and the response","The model, the reward and the punishment","Attention, retention and production","Status, power and similarity"],"e":"Factors: <b>the observer</b> (attentive? can they retain?), <b>the model</b> (someone you'd want to imitate?), and <b>the outcomes</b> (rewarded or punished?) (Woolfolk et al., 2013).","w":["Classical terms.","Outcomes cover reward/punishment; observer is missing.","Requirements, not factors.","Model features."],"k":"Observer, model, outcomes.","rn":[["","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","The observer; The model; The outcomes"]]},{"id":8045,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"When is a role model most influential?","o":["High status, reliable, competent, powerful and similar","High status, reliable, competent, powerful and different","Low status, reliable, competent and similar","High status, unreliable, powerful and similar","High status, reliable, competent, and less powerful"],"e":"Role models are most influential when they have <b>high status</b> (e.g. consultants for junior doctors), are <b>reliable</b> and <b>competent</b>, are <b>powerful</b>, and are <b>similar</b> (why representation matters in teaching).","w":["Similar.","High status.","Reliable.","Powerful."],"k":"Influential models: status, reliable, competent, powerful, similar.","rn":[["","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","Powerful; Similar; MNEMONIC"],["","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","High status; Reliable and competent"]]},{"id":8046,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Why is representation (role models learners identify with) valuable in teaching?","o":["People learn more from role models they identify with","People learn more from role models who are very different","Representation increases the use of punishment in teaching","Representation replaces the need for role models","Representation helps classical conditioning in class"],"e":"Role models are more influential when <b>similar</b> to the observer; this is why <b>representation</b> is good in teaching – you are more likely to learn from people you <b>identify</b> with.","w":["Similar.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Similarity → representation matters.","rn":[["","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","We identify with them"]]},{"id":8047,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Children copy a parent’s frequent complaints of stomach pain. According to Levy et al. (2007), illness behaviours may be what kind of behaviours?","e":"Levy et al. (2007): illness behaviours may be learnt behaviours.","k":"Learnt.","a":["learnt","learned","learnt behaviours","learned behaviours","learnt behaviour"],"rn":[["","What may illness behaviours be?","What may illness behaviours be?","Learnt behaviours"]]},{"id":8048,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Benbassat (2014) is cited for which point about role modelling in medicine?","o":["Imitate good doctors but avoid picking up their bad habits","Imitate senior doctors’ behaviour as closely as possible","Avoid imitating doctors and learn from textbooks instead","Imitate only consultants, not junior doctors","Imitate good doctors and their habits without reflection"],"e":"Imitate the behaviours of <b>good doctors</b>, but be careful <b>not to pick up their bad habits</b> (Benbassat, 2014: “reflective imitation”). On placements, learn from what doctors do well and badly.","w":["Reflective imitation.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Reflective."],"k":"Reflective imitation: avoid bad habits.","rn":[["","Observational learning in medicine: what should doctors imitate?","Observational learning in medicine: what should doctors imitate?","Good doctors; Not their bad habits"],["","What must you be careful not to pick up from doctors?","What must you be careful not to pick up from doctors?","Their bad habits"]]},{"id":8049,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which responsibility of doctors is linked to observational learning?","o":["A personal responsibility to set a good example","A responsibility to reward patients who change","A responsibility to condition healthy behaviours","A responsibility to avoid being observed","No particular responsibility beyond their own care"],"e":"<b>Personal responsibility as a doctor to set a good example</b>; one day people will learn from you, and the Hippocratic Oath lecture covers teaching the next generation.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Role models matter."],"k":"Doctors as role models.","rn":[["","What personal responsibility do doctors have as role models?","What personal responsibility do doctors have as role models?","Set a good example"]]},{"id":8050,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A nursing student watches a senior nurse calm an agitated patient, practises the technique on the ward, and keeps using it because it works. Which processes are involved?","o":["Observational learning, then operant reinforcement","Classical conditioning, then observational learning","Operant punishment, then observational learning","Observational learning only, with no conditioning","Classical conditioning only"],"e":"Observational learning (social learning theory) is more intentional: watch, retain, produce. Conditioning is still involved: good consequences make the imitated behaviour more likely (operant reinforcement).","w":["No neutral stimulus is paired with a reflex here.","Nothing makes the behaviour less likely.","The good outcome reinforces it, so conditioning is also involved.","The behaviour is voluntary and shaped by consequences."],"k":"Imitate, then consequences reinforce.","rn":[["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"],["","What are the 3 theories of learning?","What are the 3 theories of learning?","Classical conditioning; Operant conditioning; Observational learning"]]},{"id":8051,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"After several painful blood tests, a child starts crying as soon as the nurse puts on gloves. Which labels are correct?","o":["Needle pain = US; gloves = CS; crying at gloves = CR","Gloves = US; needle pain = CS; crying = UR","Needle pain = CS; gloves = US; crying = CR","Needle pain = US; gloves = NS; crying at gloves = UR","Crying = US; gloves = CS; pain = CR"],"e":"The US (pain) automatically elicits the UR (crying). Repeated pairing turns the neutral gloves into a CS, and crying at the gloves alone is the CR – same behaviour, new learnt trigger.","w":["The gloves did not originally cause crying.","Pain automatically elicits a response – it is the US.","After conditioning the gloves are a CS and the crying a CR.","Crying is the response, not a stimulus."],"k":"Paired neutral cue becomes CS."},{"id":8052,"t":"Learning","s":"A dog sits and gets a treat, so it sits more often. Separately, the dog drools when it hears the treat bag rustle. Which pair of processes is shown?","o":["Operant (sitting) and classical (drooling at the rustle)","Classical (sitting) and operant (drooling at the rustle)","Operant for both sitting and drooling","Classical for both sitting and drooling","Observational (sitting) and classical (drooling)"],"e":"Sitting is a <b>voluntary behaviour</b> increased by a treat – <b>operant</b> (positive reinforcement). Drooling (involuntary) at a previously neutral sound paired with food – <b>classical</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Drooling is involuntary.","Sitting is voluntary.","No model."],"k":"Distinguish operant vs classical.","rn":[["","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical: reflex (unlearned) response; Operant: voluntary behaviour"],["","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical: stimulus with response; Operant: behaviour with consequences"],["","What are the 3 theories of learning?","What are the 3 theories of learning?","Classical conditioning; Operant conditioning; Observational learning"]]},{"id":8053,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A child is told off and loses screen time for hitting a sibling, and hits less often afterwards. Which type of consequence is the loss of screen time?","o":["Negative punishment","Positive punishment","Negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement","Classical conditioning"],"e":"Losing screen time <b>removes something good</b> (negative) and makes hitting <b>less likely</b> (punishment) = <b>negative punishment</b>. (Being told off adds something unpleasant = positive punishment.)","w":["That would be the telling-off.","Behaviour decreases.","Behaviour decreases.","Voluntary behaviour + consequence."],"k":"Remove good → negative punishment.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive: add something bad; Negative: remove something good"],["","What are the 2 types of punishment?","What are the 2 types of punishment?","Positive; Negative"]]},{"id":8054,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A patient takes a painkiller that removes a headache and takes it more readily in future. Which mechanism is this?","o":["Negative reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Classical conditioning","Observational learning"],"e":"Removing something unpleasant (pain) makes the behaviour (taking the tablet) more likely = <b>negative reinforcement</b> – similar logic to smoking relieving withdrawal.","w":["Behaviour increases.","Behaviour increases.","Not a stimulus pairing explanation.","No model involved."],"k":"Pain relief → negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","Is negative reinforcement a punishment?","No; Makes behaviour more likely"]]},{"id":8055,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A junior doctor adopts a consultant's dismissive manner with patients. Which concepts best explain and address this?","o":["High-status role models are influential; imitate reflectively","High-status role models are influential; imitate exactly","Low-status role models are influential; imitate reflectively","Classical conditioning; the manner will fade without pairing","Negative reinforcement; the manner removes stress"],"e":"Role models are most influential when they have <b>high status</b> (consultants for juniors). Benbassat (2014) warns to <b>imitate good doctors but not their bad habits</b>.","w":["Reflective imitation.","Consultants are high status.","Not stated.","Not the best fit."],"k":"High-status models + reflective imitation.","rn":[["","What is the example of a high-status role model?","What is the example of a high-status role model?","Consultants for junior doctors"],["","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","High status; Reliable and competent"]]},{"id":8056,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A patient on chemotherapy feels sick as soon as she smells the hospital’s hand gel, even before treatment. What is the hand-gel smell, and what is the nausea it triggers?","o":["Conditioned stimulus; conditioned response","Unconditioned stimulus; unconditioned response","Neutral stimulus; unconditioned response","Conditioned stimulus; unconditioned response","Reinforcer; punishment"],"e":"Like Bernstein’s chemotherapy–ice cream aversion, a previously neutral stimulus repeatedly paired with chemotherapy (US → nausea, UR) becomes a CS producing nausea (CR).","w":["The smell did not originally cause nausea.","After conditioning it is no longer neutral.","Nausea to the smell is learnt – a CR.","This is classical, not operant, conditioning."],"k":"Smell paired with chemo → CS → CR.","rn":[["","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Associated with a response; e.g. the light"],["","CR","CR","Conditioned response"],["","Why is classical conditioning called associative?","Why is classical conditioning called associative?","A response becomes associated; With a stimulus"]]},{"id":8057,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which observation from the Bobo doll study best supports the importance of “the model” as a factor?","o":["Children copied same-gender adults more","Children remembered what the adults did","Children had the doll available to hit","Children were not rewarded for copying","Children paid attention to the adults"],"e":"The gender finding shows children <b>did not copy just anyone</b>: the characteristics of <b>the model</b> (e.g. similarity) mattered.","w":["Retention.","Production.","Outcomes.","Observer."],"k":"Model characteristics matter.","rn":[["","Which gender differences were found in the Bobo doll study?","Which gender differences were found in the Bobo doll study?","Boys copied adult men more; Girls copied adult women more"],["","What did children do alone with the Bobo doll?","What did children do alone with the Bobo doll?","Copied the adults; Beat it up"]]},{"id":8058,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A clinic wants to reduce missed appointments. Option 1 fines patients who miss; option 2 removes an annoying reminder text once the appointment is confirmed. Which pair is correct?","o":["Fine: punishment of missing; removing texts: negative reinforcement of confirming","Fine: negative reinforcement; removing texts: positive punishment","Fine: positive reinforcement; removing texts: negative punishment","Both are positive reinforcement","Both are classical conditioning"],"e":"Positive = adding, negative = taking away; reinforcement makes behaviour more likely, punishment less likely. A fine punishes missing; removing an unpleasant reminder when patients confirm negatively reinforces confirming.","w":["A fine makes missing less likely (punishment); removing something bad makes confirming more likely.","A fine is not a reward.","Fines make missing less likely.","Both are consequences of voluntary behaviour (operant)."],"k":"Classify: added/removed? more/less likely?","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"]]},{"id":8059,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Does classical conditioning still work if the person is aware of it?","o":["It seems possible it still works when aware","It never works if you are aware of the process","Awareness always makes conditioning stronger","Only animals can be conditioned in this way","Awareness turns it into a form of operant conditioning"],"e":"Students suggested you must attend to the stimulus on some level, but some responses (e.g. salivating) may occur anyway. Conclusion: it <b>seems possible</b> you'd still get the response even when conscious of it.","w":["Possible.","Not stated.","Humans too.","Not stated."],"k":"Conditioning may work despite awareness.","rn":[["","Why might very small placebos work better?","Why might very small placebos work better?","Seem scientific, latest technology"]]},{"id":8060,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A sugary drink tax rises and fizzy drink sales fall. A child also stops drinking cola after seeing a sports star refuse it. Which two learning models are illustrated?","o":["Operant (tax) and observational (role model)","Classical (tax) and observational (role model)","Operant (tax) and classical (role model)","Operant (tax) and operant (role model)","Observational (tax) and classical (role model)"],"e":"A tax is a <b>consequence</b> that makes buying sugary drinks less likely (operant). Copying a sports star is <b>observational learning</b> from a high-status, admired role model.","w":["Tax is a consequence.","Role model is observational.","Copying is observational.","Not stated."],"k":"Tax = operant; role model = observational.","rn":[["","Who is imitated in observational learning?","Who is imitated in observational learning?","A role model"],["","Match each theory to its medical example","Match each theory to its medical example","Classical: chemo and ice cream; Operant: smoking; Observational: illness behaviours, role models"],["","Bad consequences of imitating a role model is an example of what?","Bad consequences of imitating a role model is an example of what?","Operant conditioning"]]},{"id":8061,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A hospital canteen gives a free fruit pot to staff who use the stairs, and stair use rises. What is this?","o":["Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Classical conditioning"],"e":"A good thing (fruit) is <b>added</b> and the behaviour (stair use) becomes <b>more likely</b> = <b>positive reinforcement</b>, like stickers after jabs or food for Skinner's pigeon.","w":["Nothing bad is removed.","Behaviour increases.","Behaviour increases.","It is a consequence of voluntary behaviour."],"k":"Add good → ↑ behaviour.","rn":[["","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive: add something good; Negative: remove something bad"],["","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Both add something; Reinforcement: adds good; Punishment: adds bad"],["","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Positive reinforcement; Rewards reinforce behaviour"]]},{"id":8062,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A car alarm-style beep continues until the driver fastens their seatbelt; drivers start fastening it immediately. What is this?","o":["Negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement","Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Observational learning"],"e":"Fastening the belt <b>removes</b> an unpleasant noise (negative), making fastening <b>more likely</b> (reinforcement) – like the lever that stops an unpleasant noise.","w":["Nothing good added.","Behaviour increases.","Behaviour increases.","No model."],"k":"Remove unpleasant noise → negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","What is an example of negative reinforcement?","What is an example of negative reinforcement?","Pressing a lever; Stops an unpleasant noise"],["","Pressing a lever stops an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Pressing a lever stops an unpleasant noise is an example of what?","Negative reinforcement"],["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"]]},{"id":8063,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A teenager's phone is confiscated after they miss their insulin dose, and they miss fewer doses afterwards. What is the confiscation?","o":["Negative punishment","Positive punishment","Negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement","Classical conditioning"],"e":"Something good (phone) is <b>taken away</b> after the unwanted behaviour (missing doses), which becomes <b>less likely</b> = <b>negative punishment</b>.","w":["Nothing bad added.","The unwanted behaviour decreases.","Unwanted behaviour decreases.","Voluntary behaviour + consequence."],"k":"Remove good → negative punishment.","rn":[["","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive: add something bad; Negative: remove something good"],["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","Negative Punishment","Negative Punishment","Taking away something good"]]},{"id":8064,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A patient gets a painful side effect every time they take a tablet late, and they start taking it on time. Focusing on the late-taking behaviour, what is the side effect?","o":["Positive punishment","Negative punishment","Positive reinforcement","Negative reinforcement","A conditioned stimulus"],"e":"Something bad is <b>added</b> after late-taking, making late-taking <b>less likely</b> = <b>positive punishment</b>.","w":["Nothing removed.","Late-taking decreases.","Late-taking decreases.","Not classical."],"k":"Add bad → positive punishment.","rn":[["","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive: add something bad; Negative: remove something good"],["","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Both add something; Reinforcement: adds good; Punishment: adds bad"],["","Positive Punishment","Positive Punishment","Adding something bad"]]},{"id":8065,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Which example is correctly classified?","o":["Smoking to relieve withdrawal – negative reinforcement","Stickers after jabs – negative reinforcement","Fines for missed appointments – positive reinforcement","Smoking to feel good – negative reinforcement","Food for a pigeon’s turn – negative punishment"],"e":"Smoking to relieve withdrawal = <b>negative reinforcement</b>. Stickers = positive reinforcement; fines = punishment; sugar tax aims to reduce behaviour; food for turning = positive reinforcement.","w":["Positive reinforcement.","Punishment.","Positive reinforcement.","Positive reinforcement."],"k":"Classify common examples.","rn":[["","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Negative reinforcement"]]},{"id":8066,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A patient who had painful injections in a particular clinic now feels anxious entering the clinic waiting room. Which labels are correct?","o":["Injection = US; waiting room = CS; anxiety there = CR","Injection = CS; waiting room = US; anxiety there = CR","Injection = US; waiting room = CS; anxiety there = UR","Injection = US; waiting room = NS; anxiety there = CR","Anxiety = US; waiting room = CS; injection = CR"],"e":"The <b>injection</b> automatically causes the response (US → UR). The <b>waiting room</b>, previously neutral, has been paired with it and now triggers anxiety (CS → CR).","w":["Reversed.","Response to CS is CR.","No longer neutral.","Wrong."],"k":"Apply NS/US/UR/CS/CR to new examples."},{"id":8067,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Before any conditioning, what is a stimulus called that does not cause the response in question (e.g. a light, before it is paired with food)?","e":"A neutral stimulus (NS) does not cause the response; after pairing with the US it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS).","k":"Neutral stimulus.","a":["neutral stimulus","ns","neutral"],"rn":[["","Neutral stimulus (NS)","Neutral stimulus (NS)","Does not cause; The response in question; e.g. a light"],["","What is the neutral stimulus paired with?","What is the neutral stimulus paired with?","The unconditioned stimulus"],["","NS","NS","Neutral stimulus"]]},{"id":8068,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"In the classic conditioning example, which is the neutral stimulus and which is the unconditioned stimulus?","o":["NS: a light; US: food","NS: food; US: a light","NS: salivating; US: a light","NS: a bowl; US: salivating","NS: a bell; US: salivating"],"e":"Slide examples: <b>NS = a light</b>, <b>US = food</b>, <b>UR = salivating</b>, <b>CS = the light</b> (after conditioning), <b>CR = salivating</b>.","w":["Swapped.","Salivating is a response.","Salivating is a response.","Salivating is a response."],"k":"Slide examples for terms.","rn":[["","Unconditioned vs conditioned stimulus in the light example","Unconditioned vs conditioned stimulus in the light example","US: food; CS: light"],["","Image card: Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Which stage of classical conditioning is this? What is happening?","Before conditioning; US (food) causes UR (salivation); NS (light) causes no response"],["","During conditioning: what happens?","During conditioning: what happens?","CS (light) paired with; US (food); US causes UR"]]},{"id":8069,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Which statement about the unconditioned stimulus is correct?","o":["It elicits a response without conditioning","It elicits a response only after repeated pairing with another stimulus","It is the stimulus that becomes conditioned","It elicits a response only if rewarded","It is a stimulus that elicits no response"],"e":"US: ‘a stimulus that <b>automatically elicits a response</b>’ (Nolen-Hoeksema, p. 227). No conditioning has happened; it causes the reflex response.","w":["That describes the conditioned stimulus.","That is the NS.","Not operant.","That is a neutral stimulus."],"k":"US automatically elicits a response.","rn":[["","Unconditioned stimulus (US)","Unconditioned stimulus (US)","Automatically elicits a response; e.g. food"],["","What does an unconditioned stimulus do?","What does an unconditioned stimulus do?","Automatically elicits a response"]]},{"id":8070,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"What does it mean that the conditioned response is “contingent on” the conditioned stimulus?","o":["The response depends on the conditioned stimulus being present","The response happens whether or not the stimulus is present","The response causes the conditioned stimulus to appear","The response depends on a reward being given","The response depends on the unconditioned stimulus"],"e":"CR = a response <b>contingent on</b> (dependent on) the conditioned stimulus – e.g. salivating in response to the light.","w":["Dependent.","Reversed.","Not operant.","That is the UR."],"k":"CR depends on the CS.","rn":[["","Conditioned response (CR)","Conditioned response (CR)","Response contingent on the CS; e.g. salivating"],["","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Associated with a response; e.g. the light"],["","Can classical conditioning work if you are conscious of it?","Can classical conditioning work if you are conscious of it?","Possibly yes; May depend on the response"]]},{"id":8071,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A child develops a fear of the dentist's drill sound after painful treatment. To reduce the fear, the dentist plays the sound repeatedly without any pain. Which concept from the class discussion is being used?","o":["Associations can be unconditioned","Positive reinforcement of calm behaviour","Observational learning from calm children","Negative punishment of fearful behaviour","Production in observational learning"],"e":"The class concluded associations <b>can be unconditioned as well as conditioned</b>; presenting the sound without pain aims to weaken the association.","w":["Different.","Not modelling.","Different.","Different."],"k":"Unconditioning an association.","rn":[["","Why is the unconditioned response called \"unconditioned\"?","Why is the unconditioned response called \"unconditioned\"?","It is natural; No association needed"],["","Can a conditioned association be lost?","Can a conditioned association be lost?","Yes, it can be forgotten; Unconditioned as well as conditioned"]]},{"id":8072,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A parent wants a child to brush their teeth. Which approach uses observational learning most directly?","o":["The parent brushes their own teeth while the child watches","The parent gives a sticker each time the child brushes","The parent removes sweets if the child refuses to brush","The parent plays a song each time the toothbrush appears","The parent charges pocket money for missed brushing"],"e":"Observational learning: the child <b>observes and imitates</b> a role model (parent), with attention, retention, production and motivation. Stickers and removing sweets are operant; pairing with a song is classical.","w":["Positive reinforcement.","Negative punishment.","Classical.","Punishment."],"k":"Modelling = observational learning."},{"id":8073,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Children watched an adult hit an inflatable doll; later, alone with the doll, they hit it too, and boys copied men more and girls copied women more. Which factor affecting observational learning does the gender pattern show?","o":["The model (similarity)","The outcomes (reward)","The observer (attention)","Production","Retention"],"e":"Bandura et al. (1961): factors are the observer, the model and the outcomes. Role models are most influential when high status, reliable/competent, powerful and similar – children copied same-gender adults more.","w":["Children were not rewarded in this pattern.","Attention is about the observer watching.","Production is being able to replicate the behaviour.","Retention is remembering it."],"k":"Similar models are copied more.","rn":[["","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","We identify with them"],["","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","Powerful; Similar; MNEMONIC"],["","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","The observer; The model; The outcomes"]]},{"id":8074,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A medical student on placement asks “how would I have handled that differently?” after watching a consultation. Which learning process and benefit is this?","o":["Observational learning – from what doctors do well and badly","Observational learning – copying all of a senior’s behaviour","Classical conditioning – linking consultations with feelings","Operant conditioning – reinforced by praise from the doctor","Negative reinforcement – removing the stress of consulting"],"e":"On placements you observe doctors and learn from what they do <b>well</b> and <b>badly</b> (“how would you have handled it differently?”) – <b>observational/vicarious</b> learning with reflection.","w":["Reflective.","Not pairing.","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Placement learning = observation + reflection.","rn":[["","Learning from doctors on placement is an example of which theory?","Learning from doctors on placement is an example of which theory?","Observational learning"],["","What should you ask when a doctor does something badly?","What should you ask when a doctor does something badly?","How would I do differently?"]]},{"id":8075,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A patient's family all report frequent stomach pains, and the child begins to do so too. Which reference best supports a learning explanation?","o":["Levy et al. (2007): illness behaviours may be learnt","Bernstein (1991): learnt aversions after chemotherapy","Bandura et al. (1961): copying aggression","Benbassat (2014): reflective imitation of doctors","Nolen-Hoeksema (2014): definition of learning"],"e":"<b>Levy et al. (2007)</b>: <b>illness behaviours may be learnt behaviours</b> (social learning contributions to functional abdominal pain and IBD).","w":["Different study.","Related, but not illness behaviour.","About doctors.","Definition only."],"k":"Illness behaviours learnt (Levy 2007).","rn":[["","Which conditions did Levy et al. (2007) study?","Which conditions did Levy et al. (2007) study?","Functional abdominal pain; Inflammatory bowel disease"],["","What may illness behaviours be?","What may illness behaviours be?","Learnt behaviours"]]},{"id":8076,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A smoking cessation programme wants to tackle both reasons people keep smoking. Which pair of mechanisms must it address?","o":["Positive reinforcement (good feeling) and negative reinforcement (withdrawal relief)","Positive punishment (bad taste) and negative reinforcement (withdrawal relief)","Positive reinforcement (good feeling) and negative punishment (cost of cigarettes)","Classical conditioning (cues) and positive punishment (coughing)","Observational learning (friends) and positive punishment (fines)"],"e":"Smoking is maintained by <b>positive reinforcement</b> (feels good) and <b>negative reinforcement</b> (avoids withdrawal). Both increase smoking.","w":["Both reinforcement.","Both reinforcement.","Not the two.","Not the two."],"k":"Smoking: positive + negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","Smoking makes someone feel good is an example of what?","Smoking makes someone feel good is an example of what?","Positive reinforcement"],["","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Not smoking leads to withdrawal is an example of what?","Negative reinforcement"]]},{"id":8077,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A new junior doctor copies a well-respected, competent senior registrar who is similar in age. Which features make this role model influential?","o":["High status, competence and similarity","High status, power and difference","Low status, competence and similarity","High status, unreliability and similarity","Competence, similarity and being punished"],"e":"Role models are most influential when they have <b>high status</b>, are <b>reliable and competent</b>, <b>powerful</b>, and <b>similar</b>.","w":["Similar.","High status.","Reliable.","Outcomes."],"k":"Influential models.","rn":[["","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","High status; Reliable and competent"],["","What is the example of a high-status role model?","What is the example of a high-status role model?","Consultants for junior doctors"]]},{"id":8078,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Children who watch a hand-washing video but have forgotten it by the next day fail to wash properly. Which requirement of observational learning failed?","e":"Attention, retention, production, motivation: forgetting is a failure of retention.","k":"Retention.","a":["retention","remembering","memory"],"rn":[["","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","Attention; Retention; Production; Motivation; MNEMONIC"],["","Retention","Retention","Remember the behaviour"],["","How did the hand-washing song help retention?","How did the hand-washing song help retention?","The song helped children remember"]]},{"id":8079,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A health campaign uses a song so children remember the hand-washing steps. Which requirement does the song mainly target?","o":["Retention","Production","Motivation","Similarity of the model","Classical conditioning of salivation"],"e":"The Wiggles' song helped children <b>remember</b> how to wash their hands – <b>retention</b>.","w":["Doing the behaviour.","Reinforcement.","Model factor.","Not relevant."],"k":"Songs aid retention.","rn":[["","How did the hand-washing song help retention?","How did the hand-washing song help retention?","The song helped children remember"]]},{"id":8080,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which example best illustrates “the outcomes” as a factor in observational learning?","o":["A child sees a sibling rewarded for tidying","A child copies a same-gender parent tidying up","A child cannot reach the shelf to tidy","A child forgets the steps of tidying","A child is not watching when the sibling tidies up"],"e":"<b>The outcomes</b>: is the observed behaviour <b>rewarded</b> or <b>punished</b>? Seeing a sibling rewarded makes imitation more likely (vicarious reinforcement).","w":["The model.","Production.","Retention.","Attention (observer)."],"k":"Outcomes: observed reward/punishment."},{"id":8081,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement about learning across a medical career is correct?","o":["Learning is lifelong: know how you learn and what blocks it","Learning is lifelong: rely mainly on reading guidelines","Learning ends with the degree; then experience takes over","Learning matters mainly before specialty training","Learning is lifelong, but biases rarely affect it"],"e":"<b>Lifelong learning</b>: you won't stop after your degree; you'll keep learning from consultants and should know how you learn and what obstacles (e.g. biases) stop you learning from certain people.","w":["Not stated.","Lifelong.","Lifelong.","Biases matter."],"k":"Lifelong learning and biases.","rn":[["","Do you stop learning after your degree?","Do you stop learning after your degree?","No; Keep learning from consultants"]]},{"id":8082,"t":"Learning","s":"Why should doctors check their prejudices when learning from colleagues?","o":["Biases can stop us learning from certain people","Biases improve learning by focusing attention","Biases are a type of reinforcement","Biases are needed for classical conditioning","Biases affect patients but not doctors"],"e":"We all have <b>biases</b> that can stop us learning from certain people (e.g. if we think we know more), so check your prejudices – linked to GMC guidance on learning from others.","w":["Not stated.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Everyone."],"k":"Biases block learning.","rn":[["","What can stop us learning from certain people?","What can stop us learning from certain people?","Our biases"]]},{"id":8083,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A medical student watches a consultant explain a diagnosis, remembers it well, but cannot reproduce the explanation because she lacks the vocabulary. Which requirement of observational learning has failed?","e":"Observational learning needs attention (watch), retention (remember), production (replicate) and motivation (reinforcement). She attended and retained but could not produce the behaviour.","k":"Production.","a":["production","reproduction","producing","motor reproduction"],"rn":[["","Production","Production","Replicate the behaviour"],["","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","What are the 4 requirements of observational learning?","Attention; Retention; Production; Motivation; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":8085,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which of the following best explains why a doctor's own unhealthy habits matter?","o":["Patients and juniors may copy doctors as role models","Patients copy doctors only if the doctors are rewarded","Doctors’ habits are unconditioned responses","Doctors’ habits affect only their own health","Role models influence children but not adults"],"e":"Doctors are high-status role models; there is a <b>personal responsibility to set a good example</b>. The lecturer noted his own habits were learnt from the people around him.","w":["Not stated.","Learnt.","They can influence others.","Not stated."],"k":"Doctors model behaviour.","rn":[["","What is the example of a high-status role model?","What is the example of a high-status role model?","Consultants for junior doctors"],["","What personal responsibility do doctors have as role models?","What personal responsibility do doctors have as role models?","Set a good example"]]},{"id":8086,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which pair of studies are matched with their learning type correctly?","o":["Bernstein (1991) – classical; Bandura et al. (1961) – observational","Bernstein (1991) – operant; Bandura et al. (1961) – observational","Bernstein (1991) – classical; Bandura et al. (1961) – operant","Bernstein (1991) – observational; Bandura et al. (1961) – classical","Bernstein (1991) – operant; Bandura et al. (1961) – classical"],"e":"Bernstein: chemotherapy–ice cream aversion (<b>classical</b>). Bandura: Bobo doll (<b>observational</b>).","w":["Classical.","Observational.","Swapped.","Swapped."],"k":"Match studies to learning types.","rn":[["","What is the example study of observational learning?","What is the example study of observational learning?","Bobo doll study; (Bandura et al., 1961)"]]},{"id":8087,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement about intentionality across the three models is consistent with the comparison table?","o":["Classical and operant: associative, less intentional; observational: more","Classical and operant: intentional; observational: associative, less","All three are equally intentional","Classical is the most intentional of the three","Operant involves no association at all"],"e":"Classical (less intentional, associative); operant (associative, consequence-driven); observational (<b>more intentional</b>, as a student pointed out and the lecturer agreed).","w":["Reversed.","Not equal.","Least.","It is associative."],"k":"Intentionality differs across models.","rn":[["","What do classical and operant conditioning have in common?","What do classical and operant conditioning have in common?","Both are associative learning; Both less intentional"],["","Is associative learning more or less intentional?","Is associative learning more or less intentional?","Less intentional"]]},{"id":8088,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Learning by doing a behaviour yourself and seeing how it works out is called what type of learning?","e":"Enactive learning: performing the behaviour and experiencing its outcome; vicarious learning: seeing others and their consequences.","k":"Enactive.","a":["enactive","enactive learning"],"rn":[["","What are 2 other names for learning by observation?","What are 2 other names for learning by observation?","Vicarious learning; Enactive learning"],["","Enactive learning","Enactive learning","Imitating (enacting) a behaviour; Seeing how it works out"],["","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Vicarious; Enactive"]]},{"id":8089,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Learning from seeing other people’s behaviour and its consequences is called what type of learning?","e":"Vicarious learning: learning from others’ behaviour and consequences (social learning theory).","k":"Vicarious.","a":["vicarious","vicarious learning","observational learning","observational"],"rn":[["","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","By observation (vicarious learning)"],["","Vicarious Learning","Vicarious Learning","Learning by watching others' behaviour"],["","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Which 2 learning types does social learning theory name?","Vicarious; Enactive"]]},{"id":8090,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A patient who had unpleasant chemotherapy now feels nauseated in the hospital car park. What is the car park? (two words)","e":"A previously neutral stimulus paired with chemotherapy becomes a conditioned stimulus that triggers nausea (CR).","k":"Conditioned stimulus.","a":["conditioned stimulus","cs"],"rn":[["","Classical conditioning","Classical conditioning","A neutral stimulus; Becomes associated with another stimulus; Through repeated pairing"],["","What does the neutral stimulus turn into after conditioning?","What does the neutral stimulus turn into after conditioning?","A conditioned stimulus"],["","Why is classical conditioning called associative?","Why is classical conditioning called associative?","A response becomes associated; With a stimulus"]]},{"id":8091,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Which of the following is not an example of operant conditioning in medicine or public health?","o":["Chemotherapy and ice cream aversion","Stickers after injections","Smoking and withdrawal","Fines for missed GP appointments","Sugar taxes on fizzy drinks"],"e":"The chemotherapy–ice cream aversion is <b>classical</b> conditioning (Bernstein, 1991). The others are operant examples.","w":["Operant.","Operant.","Operant.","Operant."],"k":"Chemo aversion = classical.","rn":[["","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","Aversion to ice cream"],["","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Linked ice cream with; Chemo nausea"],["","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","Chemotherapy nausea; And ice cream; (Bernstein, 1991)"]]},{"id":8092,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Which statement about punishment is correct?","o":["Makes behaviour less likely; positive or negative","Makes behaviour less likely; always adds something bad","Makes behaviour more likely; positive or negative","Makes behaviour less likely; includes negative reinforcement","Makes behaviour less likely; only in classical conditioning"],"e":"<b>Punishment</b> makes a behaviour <b>less likely</b>; it can be <b>positive</b> (adding something bad) or <b>negative</b> (taking away something good).","w":["Can remove good.","Less likely.","Reinforcement.","Operant."],"k":"Punishment: ↓ behaviour; + or −.","rn":[["","Punishment","Punishment","Makes the behaviour less likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"]]},{"id":8093,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Which statement about reinforcement is correct?","o":["Makes behaviour more likely; positive or negative","Makes behaviour more likely; always adds a reward","Makes behaviour less likely; positive or negative","Makes behaviour more likely; only negative","Makes behaviour more likely; only in classical conditioning"],"e":"<b>Reinforcement</b> makes behaviour <b>more likely</b>: <b>positive</b> (add good) or <b>negative</b> (remove bad).","w":["Can remove bad.","More likely.","Both.","Operant."],"k":"Reinforcement: ↑ behaviour; + or −.","rn":[["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","Punishment","Punishment","Makes the behaviour less likely; Can be positive or negative"]]},{"id":8094,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A nurse praises a patient every time they complete their physiotherapy exercises, and adherence improves. A role-model patient in the ward also visibly benefits from exercising. Which processes are at work?","o":["Positive reinforcement and observational learning","Negative reinforcement and observational learning","Positive reinforcement and classical conditioning","Positive punishment and observational learning","Negative punishment and classical conditioning"],"e":"Praise adds something good → <b>positive reinforcement</b>. Seeing a fellow patient benefit is <b>observational learning</b> with a similar role model and good outcomes.","w":["Praise is added.","Role model.","Praise adds good.","Wrong."],"k":"Combine operant and observational learning.","rn":[["","Who is imitated in observational learning?","Who is imitated in observational learning?","A role model"],["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"],["","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Positive reinforcement vs positive punishment","Both add something; Reinforcement: adds good; Punishment: adds bad"]]},{"id":8095,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which of the following would most reduce a child's imitation of a model's behaviour?","o":["Seeing the model punished for it","Seeing the model rewarded for it","A model who is similar to the child","A model who has high status","Paying close attention to the model"],"e":"Under “the outcomes”, if the observed behaviour is <b>punished</b>, the observer is less likely to repeat it.","w":["Increases.","Increases.","Increases.","Increases."],"k":"Observed punishment ↓ imitation.","rn":[["","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","The observer; The model; The outcomes"],["","Is there conditioning within observational learning?","Is there conditioning within observational learning?","Yes, partly; Imitation gets rewarded or punished"],["","Who is imitated in observational learning?","Who is imitated in observational learning?","A role model"]]},{"id":8096,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A newly qualified doctor copies the senior registrar who is respected, competent and the same age as her, rather than an anxious locum. Which feature list best explains her choice?","o":["High status, competent, similar","Low status, competent, different","High status, unreliable, similar","Powerful, incompetent, different","Low status, reliable, different"],"e":"Role models are most influential when they have high status, are reliable and competent, powerful, and similar to the observer.","w":["Low status and dissimilar models are less influential.","Unreliable models are less influential.","Incompetent and dissimilar models are less influential.","Low status and dissimilarity reduce influence."],"k":"Status, competence, power, similarity.","rn":[["","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","High status; Reliable and competent"],["","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","Powerful; Similar; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":8097,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Which statement about Pavlov's bells is correct?","o":["Bell with food eventually made the bell alone cause salivation","Ringing a bell with food made the bell an unconditioned stimulus for salivation","Ringing a bell after salivation rewarded the dog for drooling","Ringing a bell alone caused salivation from the start","Ringing a bell punished the dog for not salivating"],"e":"Pavlov fed the dog while ringing a bell; eventually the <b>bell alone</b> caused salivation – the bell became a <b>CS</b> and salivation to it a <b>CR</b>.","w":["The bell became a CS.","Not operant.","Neutral at first.","Not punishment."],"k":"Pavlov's bell → CS.","rn":[["","What did Pavlov pair with food?","What did Pavlov pair with food?","Ringing bells"],["","After conditioning: what happens?","After conditioning: what happens?","CS (light) alone; Causes CR (salivation)"]]},{"id":8098,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement about learning and behaviour change in patients is correct?","o":["Getting patients to change unhealthy behaviours is learning","Behaviour change in patients is unrelated to learning","Only children can learn new health behaviours","Information alone rarely changes behaviour","Patients cannot unlearn unhealthy behaviours"],"e":"One job of a doctor is to get patients to learn from you, e.g. change unhealthy behaviours; that <b>behaviour change is the learning process</b>.","w":["It is learning.","Not stated.","Information can.","Not stated."],"k":"Behaviour change = learning.","rn":[["","Why does behaviour change matter when a doctor gives information?","Why does behaviour change matter when a doctor gives information?","Behaviour change is; The learning process"]]},{"id":8099,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"What is the conditioning phase example?","o":["The light is switched on when food is present","The light is shown alone with no food","Food is given without the light","The dog is rewarded for sitting by the light","The light is switched off when food is present"],"e":"Conditioning phase example: <b>the light is switched on when food is present</b> – the NS paired with the US.","w":["After conditioning.","Before conditioning.","Operant.","On with food."],"k":"Conditioning phase example.","rn":[["","What is an example of the conditioning phase?","What is an example of the conditioning phase?","Light switched on; When food is present"]]},{"id":8100,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"What does the abbreviation CS stand for?","o":["Conditioned stimulus","Classical stimulus","Conditioned salivation","Clinical sign","Contingent signal"],"e":"<b>CS = conditioned stimulus</b>: a stimulus that has become associated with a response. CR = conditioned response; NS = neutral stimulus; US/UR = unconditioned stimulus/response.","w":["Not used.","Not used.","Not used.","Not used."],"k":"CS = conditioned stimulus.","rn":[["","CS","CS","Conditioned stimulus"],["","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Conditioned stimulus (CS)","Associated with a response; e.g. the light"]]},{"id":8101,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A patient is anxious about needles after seeing their mother faint during a blood test. Which learning type best explains the anxiety's origin?","o":["Observational learning","Classical conditioning to needles","Positive reinforcement","Negative punishment","An unconditioned reflex"],"e":"The fear was acquired by <b>observing</b> another person's experience – <b>vicarious/observational</b> learning, like fears being learnt behaviours.","w":["The fear came from observing, not direct pairing.","Not consequences.","Not consequences.","Learnt."],"k":"Fears can be learnt vicariously.","rn":[["","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","How was the behaviour first learnt in observational learning?","By observation (vicarious learning)"]]},{"id":8102,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which of the following best explains why “representation” and similarity matter in medical teaching?","o":["Learners are influenced by role models they identify with","Learners are more influenced by role models unlike them","Similarity reduces attention to the role model","Representation is irrelevant to how observational learning works","Similarity strengthens classical conditioning"],"e":"Role models are more influential when <b>similar</b>; you are more likely to learn from people you <b>identify</b> with.","w":["Similar.","Not stated.","It matters.","Not stated."],"k":"Similarity enhances role-model influence.","rn":[["","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","Why does similarity make a role model influential?","We identify with them"]]},{"id":8103,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement correctly contrasts classical and operant conditioning?","o":["Classical links a reflex to a new stimulus; operant shapes voluntary acts by consequences","Classical shapes voluntary acts by consequences; operant links a reflex to a new stimulus","Both shape voluntary behaviour through rewards","Classical needs a role model; operant needs pairing","Both are forms of observational learning"],"e":"Classical (Pavlovian): a neutral stimulus paired with an US comes to trigger the response. Operant (instrumental): voluntary behaviour is modified by its consequences. Both are associative learning.","w":["Reversed.","Classical conditioning concerns involuntary responses.","Role models belong to observational learning.","Both are associative learning, not observational."],"k":"Classical = pairing; operant = consequences.","rn":[["","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical: reflex (unlearned) response; Operant: voluntary behaviour"],["","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical: stimulus with response; Operant: behaviour with consequences"],["","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Learning to modify voluntary behaviours; In light of consequences"]]},{"id":8104,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A teenager starts vaping after seeing popular older friends do it, and keeps vaping because it relieves the irritability he feels without it. Which two mechanisms explain starting and continuing?","o":["Observational learning; negative reinforcement","Classical conditioning; positive punishment","Positive reinforcement; observational learning","Negative punishment; classical conditioning","Observational learning; negative punishment"],"e":"Starting by copying high-status, similar peers is observational learning; relief of withdrawal-like irritability is negative reinforcement (taking away something bad makes the behaviour more likely), as with smoking.","w":["He started by copying, and continuing is driven by relief.","Copying comes first; relief maintains it.","Punishment makes behaviour less likely.","Removing irritability makes vaping more likely – reinforcement."],"k":"Start by copying; continue via relief.","rn":[["","Negative Reinforcement","Negative Reinforcement","Taking away something bad"],["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"],["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"]]},{"id":8105,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"What is meant by the claim that the three models each have “insights”?","o":["Each has something right and maybe something wrong","Only one of the three theories is correct","All three theories are wrong in medicine","The three theories are really the same theory","Only Skinner’s theory has real insights for doctors"],"e":"As with the language-development theories, there is something right and maybe something wrong about each theory; look for their <b>insights</b>.","w":["Each has insights.","Not stated.","Different.","Each has insights."],"k":"Use insights from each model.","rn":[["","What is true of every theory of learning?","What is true of every theory of learning?","Something right about each; Something wrong about each"]]},{"id":8106,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A dog does a trick and gets a treat, so it keeps doing the trick. What is the behaviour and the consequence example?","o":["Behaviour = new trick; consequence = treat","Behaviour = treat; consequence = new trick","Behaviour = salivation; consequence = food","Behaviour = sitting; consequence = bell","Behaviour = new trick; consequence = salivation"],"e":"Slide example of operant conditioning: <b>behaviour = new trick</b>; <b>consequence = treat</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Classical.","Not the example.","The treat."],"k":"Trick → treat.","rn":[["","What is the example of operant conditioning?","What is the example of operant conditioning?","Behaviour: new trick; Consequence: treat"],["","In the dog trick example, what is the consequence?","In the dog trick example, what is the consequence?","A treat"]]},{"id":8107,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which pairing of observational learning factor and question is correct?","o":["The observer – attentive, and able to retain it?","The model – is the behaviour rewarded?","The outcomes – someone you’d want to imitate?","The observer – high status and powerful?","The outcomes – attentive, and able to retain it?"],"e":"<b>Observer</b>: attentive? can they retain? <b>Model</b>: someone you'd want to imitate? <b>Outcomes</b>: rewarded or punished?","w":["Outcomes.","Model.","Model.","Observer."],"k":"Factor–question pairing.","rn":[["","What questions does \"the observer\" factor ask?","What questions does \"the observer\" factor ask?","Are they attentive?; Can they retain the information?"]]},{"id":8108,"t":"Learning","s":"Which statement best summarises why clinicians need learning theory?","o":["Learning changes behaviour; unhealthy habits are learnt","Learning changes knowledge; unhealthy habits are innate and fixed","Learning changes behaviour; only children can learn healthy habits","Learning changes behaviour; doctors should leave behaviour change to psychologists","Learning changes behaviour; it replaces medical treatment"],"e":"Take-home: learning is <b>a relatively permanent change in behaviour as a result of experience</b>; clinicians need to understand it because <b>unhealthy behaviours are often learnt</b> and they can help people <b>learn healthy behaviours</b>.","w":["Behaviour.","Adults too.","Doctors help patients learn.","Not stated."],"k":"Take-home message.","rn":[["","Why should clinicians learn about learning?","Why should clinicians learn about learning?","Unhealthy behaviours are learnt; Help others learn healthy behaviours"]]},{"id":8109,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A health app gives users badges for daily step goals, and step counts rise. If the app instead stopped an annoying daily reminder once the goal was met, and steps rose, which two mechanisms would these be?","o":["Badges: positive reinforcement; stopping reminder: negative reinforcement","Badges: negative reinforcement; stopping reminder: positive reinforcement","Badges: positive reinforcement; stopping reminder: negative punishment","Badges: positive punishment; stopping reminder: negative reinforcement","Badges: classical conditioning; stopping reminder: operant"],"e":"Adding badges (good) → positive reinforcement. Removing an annoying reminder (bad) → negative reinforcement. Both increase behaviour.","w":["Swapped.","Behaviour increased.","Badges add good.","Both operant."],"k":"Positive vs negative reinforcement.","rn":[["","Reinforcement","Reinforcement","Makes the behaviour more likely; Can be positive or negative"],["","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","What are the 2 types of reinforcement?","Positive; Negative"],["","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive: add something good; Negative: remove something bad"]]},{"id":8110,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Why are placebos used as comparators in drug trials, in learning terms?","o":["People may be conditioned to feel better from a pill","Placebos are more effective than most drugs","Placebos contain small doses of the active drug being tested","Placebos punish patients who drop out","Placebos are a form of observational learning"],"e":"In RCTs a drug is compared to a <b>placebo</b> (looks like the drug but does nothing), not to nothing, perhaps because people have been <b>conditioned</b> to feel better just by taking a pill.","w":["Not stated.","No active drug.","Not stated.","Classical."],"k":"Placebo comparators and conditioning.","rn":[["","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Conditioned to feel better; Just by taking a pill"]]},{"id":8111,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"Which statement is <b>false</b>?","o":["Classical conditioning modifies voluntary behaviour by consequences","Operant conditioning is also called instrumental conditioning","Classical conditioning is also called Pavlovian conditioning","Observational learning is also called social learning theory","Associative learning includes classical and operant conditioning"],"e":"Modifying <b>voluntary behaviour by consequences</b> is <b>operant</b> conditioning, not classical.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Don't mix definitions.","rn":[["","What is operant conditioning?","What is operant conditioning?","Modifying voluntary behaviours; In light of consequences"],["","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Operant (Instrumental) Conditioning","Learning to modify voluntary behaviours; In light of consequences"]]},{"id":8112,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which example is used for “production” in the Bobo doll study?","o":["Children were placed where they could hit the doll","The children watched the adults hit the doll","The children remembered what the adults did","The children copied same-gender adults","The children were rewarded by adults for hitting the doll"],"e":"Production: the children were placed where they <b>could also hit the doll</b> – able to replicate the behaviour.","w":["Attention.","Retention.","Model.","Not stated."],"k":"Bobo: production.","rn":[["","In the Bobo doll study, how was production possible?","In the Bobo doll study, how was production possible?","Children put in a room; With the doll"]]},{"id":8113,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which statement about the Bobo doll study design is correct?","o":["A controlled experiment: one group saw the doll beaten","An observational study with no comparison group","A controlled experiment comparing boys and girls only","A controlled experiment where children were punished","A study of adults copying children"],"e":"It was a <b>controlled experiment</b>: one group saw the clown beaten up, the other did not.","w":["There was a control.","Groups by exposure.","Not stated.","Children copied adults."],"k":"Bobo doll was controlled.","rn":[["","Was the Bobo doll study controlled?","Was the Bobo doll study controlled?","Yes; Doll beaten vs not beaten"]]},{"id":8114,"t":"Learning","s":"Why might a GP placement be valuable in terms of learning theory?","o":["Students observe role models and reflect on what to do differently","Students are classically conditioned to feel calm in clinics","Students are rewarded for copying the GP’s habits","Students receive less feedback, so learn independently","Students learn mainly by reading guidance on placement"],"e":"You will learn on observations in local <b>GP practices</b> and placements; doctors should be <b>good role models</b> – observational learning with reflection.","w":["Not stated.","Reflective.","Not stated.","Observation."],"k":"Placements = observational learning.","rn":[["","What should the doctors you observe on placement be?","What should the doctors you observe on placement be?","Good role models"],["","Where will students observe doctors in practice?","Where will students observe doctors in practice?","Placements; Local GP practices"]]},{"id":8115,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Which example would be classical rather than operant conditioning?","o":["Nausea at a hospital smell after treatment","Taking medication on time to earn praise from staff","Avoiding late payment after being fined","Smoking to relieve withdrawal symptoms","Using the stairs to get a free reward"],"e":"An <b>involuntary</b> response (nausea) to a previously neutral stimulus (smell) paired with treatment = <b>classical</b>. The others involve voluntary behaviour and consequences (operant).","w":["Operant.","Operant.","Operant.","Operant."],"k":"Involuntary response to paired stimulus = classical."},{"id":8116,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A child with leukaemia was given his favourite crisps after each chemotherapy session and now refuses crisps. Which intervention follows from classical conditioning?","o":["Avoid pairing favourite foods with treatment","Give the crisps before treatment as a reward","Punish refusal of the crisps","Show other children eating crisps","Increase the chemotherapy dose"],"e":"Bernstein (1991): chemotherapy (US) causes nausea (UR); food given around it becomes a CS causing aversion (CR). Avoid pairing valued foods with treatment.","w":["Any close pairing with the nausea can condition the aversion.","Punishment does not undo a conditioned aversion.","That uses observational learning, and does not address the pairing.","Not relevant."],"k":"Don’t pair favourite foods with chemo.","rn":[["","During conditioning: what happens?","During conditioning: what happens?","CS (light) paired with; US (food); US causes UR"]]},{"id":8117,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"Which statement about the role of attention in both classical conditioning and observational learning is supported?","o":["Attention seems needed for a link, and is a requirement","Attention matters for observational learning only","Attention matters for operant conditioning only","Attention prevents classical conditioning from occurring at all","Attention is a form of positive reinforcement"],"e":"In the class discussion students suggested you must be paying attention on some level to make the link; <b>attention</b> is also the first requirement of observational learning.","w":["Both.","Not stated.","Not stated.","Not reinforcement."],"k":"Attention matters across learning.","rn":[["","Why might attention be needed for classical conditioning?","Why might attention be needed for classical conditioning?","Without attending to stimulus; No link is made"],["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"]]},{"id":8118,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which public health strategy uses observational learning rather than operant conditioning?","o":["Showing respected celebrities being vaccinated","A tax on cigarettes and alcohol","Fines for missed appointments","Stickers after vaccinations","Withdrawing benefits from people who do not attend"],"e":"Showing high-status role models performing a behaviour is <b>observational learning</b>. Taxes, fines, stickers and withdrawing benefits are consequences (operant).","w":["Operant.","Operant.","Operant.","Operant."],"k":"Role-model campaigns = observational."},{"id":8119,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A child who has never seen a needle cries when they see one, because their older brother screamed during his injection. Later, the child gets a sticker for staying calm and stays calm more often. Which processes occurred, in order?","o":["Observational learning, then positive reinforcement","Classical conditioning, then positive reinforcement","Observational learning, then negative reinforcement","Positive reinforcement, then observational learning","Observational learning, then positive punishment"],"e":"The fear came from <b>observing</b> the brother (observational learning). The sticker added for calm behaviour = <b>positive reinforcement</b>.","w":["No pairing.","Sticker added.","Order.","Behaviour increased."],"k":"Sequence of learning processes.","rn":[["","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Stickers after injections are an example of what?","Positive reinforcement; Rewards reinforce behaviour"],["","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Which requirement links observational learning to operant conditioning?","Motivation (reinforcement)"]]},{"id":8120,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"Which statement best summarises the medical relevance of all three models?","o":["Classical: aversions; operant: consequences; observational: role models","Classical: role models; operant: aversions; observational: consequences","Classical: consequences; operant: role models; observational: aversions","Operant explains all three kinds of example","Classical explains all three kinds of example"],"e":"Medical examples: <b>classical</b> – chemotherapy aversion; <b>operant</b> – smoking, stickers, fines, sugar taxes; <b>observational</b> – illness behaviours, copying doctors, setting a good example.","w":["Mixed.","Mixed.","Each has insights.","Each has insights."],"k":"Medical relevance of all three models.","rn":[["","Match each theory to its medical example","Match each theory to its medical example","Classical: chemo and ice cream; Operant: smoking; Observational: illness behaviours, role models"],["","Bad consequences of imitating a role model is an example of what?","Bad consequences of imitating a role model is an example of what?","Operant conditioning"]]},{"id":8121,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A 45-year-old woman is having chemotherapy, which always makes her feel sick. After each session she eats a strawberry ice cream from the hospital café. After several cycles, she feels nauseated and avoids strawberry ice cream even on days when she has had no treatment.<br><br>In this example, what are the conditioned stimulus and the conditioned response?","o":["CS: the ice cream; CR: aversion to the ice cream","CS: chemotherapy; CR: nausea after chemotherapy","CS: the ice cream; CR: nausea caused directly by chemotherapy","CS: nausea; CR: chemotherapy","CS: chemotherapy, which has become linked with the ice cream; CR: avoiding the hospital café"],"e":"Step 1: chemotherapy is the unconditioned stimulus (US) that automatically causes nausea, the unconditioned response (UR); the ice cream starts as a neutral stimulus. Step 2: through repeated pairing with chemotherapy, the ice cream becomes the conditioned stimulus (CS), and the learned aversion to it is the conditioned response (CR) (Bernstein, 1991).","w":["Chemotherapy and the nausea it causes are the US and UR.","Nausea caused directly by chemotherapy is the UR, not the CR.","Nausea is a response, not a stimulus.","Chemotherapy is the unconditioned stimulus."],"k":"Chemo (US) → nausea (UR); ice cream (CS) → aversion (CR).","rn":[["","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Why did the children develop an aversion to ice cream?","Linked ice cream with; Chemo nausea"],["","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","In Bernstein (1991), what is the conditioned response?","Aversion to ice cream"],["","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","What is the medical example of classical conditioning?","Chemotherapy nausea; And ice cream; (Bernstein, 1991)"]]},{"id":8122,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"In a laboratory, a dog salivates when food is put in front of it. A light is then switched on every time food is given, for many days. Eventually the light is switched on with no food, and the dog salivates. The researcher writes up the experiment using the technical terms.<br><br>Which description of the light is correct before and after conditioning?","o":["Before: neutral stimulus; after: conditioned stimulus causing a CR","Before: unconditioned stimulus; after: neutral stimulus","Before: conditioned stimulus; after: unconditioned stimulus","Before: unconditioned response; after: conditioned response","Before: neutral stimulus; after: unconditioned stimulus that automatically elicits salivation"],"e":"Step 1: before conditioning, the light is a neutral stimulus (NS): it does not cause salivation, while food is the unconditioned stimulus (US) causing the unconditioned response (UR). Step 2: after the light is repeatedly paired with food (the conditioning phase), it becomes a conditioned stimulus (CS), and salivation to the light is the conditioned response (CR), the same behaviour as the UR but now learned.","w":["The light does not automatically cause salivation before conditioning.","The light only becomes a CS after pairing.","The light is a stimulus, not a response.","A learned trigger is a CS; a US needs no learning."],"k":"NS + US pairing → NS becomes CS producing CR.","rn":[["","When does a neutral stimulus start causing salivation?","When does a neutral stimulus start causing salivation?","After enough pairing (conditioning)"],["","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned vs unconditioned response: what causes it","Conditioned: the conditioned stimulus (light); Unconditioned: the unconditioned stimulus (food)"],["","Classical conditioning","Classical conditioning","A neutral stimulus; Becomes associated with another stimulus; Through repeated pairing"]]},{"id":8123,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A 38-year-old smoker tries to quit. Whenever he goes a few hours without a cigarette he becomes irritable and restless, and smoking makes these withdrawal symptoms go away. He also says that smoking makes him feel good. He finds it very hard to stop.<br><br>Which two operant processes are keeping his smoking going?","o":["Positive reinforcement (feels good) and negative reinforcement (withdrawal removed)","Positive punishment (feels good) and negative punishment (withdrawal removed)","Negative punishment (feels good) and positive reinforcement (withdrawal removed)","Classical conditioning only, because nicotine is an unconditioned stimulus","Positive punishment, because withdrawal is something bad that is added when he stops smoking"],"e":"Step 1: in operant conditioning, reinforcement makes a behaviour more likely and punishment less likely; positive means adding and negative means taking away. Step 2: smoking adds a good feeling (positive reinforcement) and takes away bad withdrawal symptoms (negative reinforcement), so both make smoking more likely.","w":["Both processes make smoking more likely, so neither is punishment.","Feeling good is added, which is positive, and both are reinforcement.","The lecture uses smoking as an operant example of reinforcement.","Withdrawal is removed by smoking, which reinforces smoking."],"k":"Smoking: positive (feel good) + negative (remove withdrawal) reinforcement.","rn":[["","Smoking makes someone feel good is an example of what?","Smoking makes someone feel good is an example of what?","Positive reinforcement"],["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive vs negative reinforcement","Positive: add something good; Negative: remove something bad"]]},{"id":8124,"t":"Operant Conditioning","s":"A parent tries several approaches with a 6-year-old. Approach 1: the child gets a sticker after a vaccination. Approach 2: the child loses screen time after hitting a sibling. Approach 3: the child is made to tidy the whole house after lying. Approach 4: the child is let off washing the dishes after finishing homework early.<br><br>Which classification is correct?","o":["1 positive reinforcement; 2 negative punishment; 3 positive punishment; 4 negative reinforcement","1 negative reinforcement; 2 positive punishment; 3 negative punishment; 4 positive reinforcement","1 positive reinforcement; 2 positive punishment; 3 negative punishment; 4 negative reinforcement","1 positive punishment; 2 negative reinforcement; 3 positive reinforcement; 4 negative punishment","All four are reinforcement because they all change behaviour"],"e":"Step 1: ask whether something is added (positive) or taken away (negative): the sticker and tidying are added; screen time and dishes are removed. Step 2: ask whether behaviour becomes more likely (reinforcement) or less likely (punishment): a sticker (good added) and being let off dishes (bad removed) reinforce, while losing screen time (good removed) and tidying (bad added) punish.","w":["A sticker is added, so it is positive reinforcement.","Losing screen time removes something good (negative punishment), and tidying adds something bad.","A sticker is added, not removed, and it rewards.","Punishment makes behaviour less likely; only 1 and 4 reinforce."],"k":"Positive = add, negative = remove; reinforcement ↑, punishment ↓.","rn":[["","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Negative reinforcement vs negative punishment","Both remove something; Reinforcement: removes bad; Punishment: removes good"],["","What are the 4 types of operant consequence?","What are the 4 types of operant consequence?","Positive reinforcement; Negative reinforcement; Positive punishment; Negative punishment; MNEMONIC"],["","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive vs negative punishment","Positive: add something bad; Negative: remove something good"]]},{"id":8125,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"In a study, nursery children watch an adult hitting and kicking an inflatable clown. Later the children are left in a room with the same clown, and many of them hit it in the same way. Children who saw the adult being rewarded copy the behaviour more than those who saw the adult being told off.<br><br>Which type of learning is shown, and what does the reward finding show?","o":["Observational (social) learning; the outcomes for the model affect imitation","Classical conditioning; the clown has become a conditioned stimulus","Operant conditioning only; the children were directly rewarded","Associative learning; reinforcement only works on the person who behaves","Observational learning; the model's outcomes have no effect, because attention and retention alone decide whether a child copies"],"e":"Step 1: learning by observing and imitating others is observational learning (social learning theory), as in the Bobo doll study (Bandura et al., 1961). Step 2: one factor affecting observational learning is the outcomes: if the behaviour is rewarded the observer is more likely to repeat it, and if punished, less likely.","w":["The clown is not paired with an unconditioned stimulus.","The children were not directly rewarded; they watched the model.","Seeing a model rewarded also affects the observer.","Outcomes are one of the factors that affect observational learning."],"k":"Bobo doll: observational learning; model's outcomes affect copying.","rn":[["","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","What are the 3 factors that affect observational learning?","The observer; The model; The outcomes"]]},{"id":8126,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"During a pandemic, a hospital shows staff a video of a senior nurse washing her hands correctly. Some staff did not watch closely, some forgot the steps by the next day, and some had no sink nearby to practise. A few watched, remembered and could do it, but saw no reason to bother as nobody noticed when they did.<br><br>Which requirement is missing in the last group?","o":["Motivation: the behaviour is not being reinforced","Attention: they did not watch the behaviour","Retention: they did not remember the behaviour","Production: they could not replicate the behaviour","Conditioning phase: the video was not paired with an unconditioned stimulus"],"e":"Step 1: observational learning needs attention (watch), retention (remember), production (replicate) and motivation (have the behaviour reinforced); the first groups lacked attention, retention and production. Step 2: the last group watched, remembered and could do it, but the behaviour was not reinforced, so motivation is missing.","w":["The last group did watch.","The last group did remember.","The last group could replicate it.","Pairing with a US belongs to classical conditioning."],"k":"Observational learning: attention, retention, production, motivation.","rn":[["","Motivation","Motivation","Have the behaviour reinforced"]]},{"id":8127,"t":"Observational Learning","s":"A hospital wants junior doctors to copy good communication habits. Option A is to have them watch a respected, competent consultant of similar background to themselves. Option B is to have them watch a junior who is often unreliable and has low status. A medical educator chooses option A and warns the juniors about one risk.<br><br>Why is option A more effective, and what is the risk?","o":["High status, competent and similar models are imitated more; juniors may copy bad habits","Unreliable models are imitated more; there is no risk","Option A uses classical conditioning; the risk is nausea","Option A uses negative punishment; the risk is that the consultant stops teaching altogether","Status does not matter; juniors copy anyone they watch, so there is no particular risk"],"e":"Step 1: a role-model is most influential when they have high status, are reliable and competent, are powerful and are similar to the observer. Step 2: doctors can learn by imitating good doctors, but must be careful not to pick up their bad habits (Benbassat, 2014), so each doctor has a personal responsibility to set a good example.","w":["Reliable and competent models are more influential.","This is observational learning, not classical conditioning.","This is observational learning, not punishment.","The model's characteristics affect how influential they are."],"k":"Influential models: high status, competent, powerful, similar; beware bad habits.","rn":[["","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","What are 2 more features of an influential role model?","Powerful; Similar; MNEMONIC"],["","What is the example of a high-status role model?","What is the example of a high-status role model?","Consultants for junior doctors"],["","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","What are 2 features of an influential role model?","High status; Reliable and competent"]]},{"id":8128,"t":"Learning","s":"A 30-year-old woman grew up watching her mother take to bed and stop working whenever she had minor aches. As an adult, she does the same with small aches, much more than her colleagues. Her GP thinks this pattern was not innate but learned from experience.<br><br>Which explanation from the lecture best fits, and what is meant by learning?","o":["Illness behaviours may be learnt by observation; learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour from experience","Illness behaviour is a reflex, so it is an unconditioned response","Illness behaviour is caused by negative punishment, which is learning without experience","Illness behaviour is a conditioned stimulus; learning is a brief change in behaviour","Illness behaviours are inborn; learning is any change, however short"],"e":"Step 1: learning is a relatively permanent change in behaviour that occurs as a result of experience (Nolen-Hoeksema, 2014), and observational learning involves imitating a role-model. Step 2: the lecture notes that illness behaviours may be learnt behaviours (Levy et al., 2007), which fits her copying her mother's response to minor aches.","w":["A learned pattern is not an unlearned reflex.","Punishment would make the behaviour less likely, and learning comes from experience.","A behaviour is not a stimulus, and learning is relatively permanent.","The GP thinks it was learned, and learning is relatively permanent."],"k":"Illness behaviours may be learnt (Levy et al., 2007).","rn":[["","Learning","Learning","Relatively permanent change; In behaviour; As a result of experience"],["","How do psychologists define learning?","How do psychologists define learning?","Relatively permanent change in behaviour; As a result of experience"]]},{"id":8129,"t":"Learning","s":"A student compares two cases. In case 1, a child gets a sticker after each injection and becomes more willing to have injections. In case 2, a child who had painful injections in a white room starts crying automatically whenever she enters any white room.<br><br>Which statement correctly contrasts the two cases?","o":["Case 1 is operant: a voluntary behaviour shaped by consequences; case 2 is classical: an involuntary response to a new stimulus","Case 1 is classical; case 2 is operant","Both are observational learning","Case 1 is classical, because the sticker is an unconditioned stimulus","Both are operant, because crying is a voluntary behaviour that the child chooses"],"e":"Step 1: operant conditioning is learning to modify voluntary behaviours in light of consequences, as with stickers after injections (positive reinforcement). Step 2: classical conditioning is when a previously neutral stimulus (the white room) becomes associated with another stimulus (painful injections) through repeated pairing and produces an involuntary conditioned response (crying).","w":["The labels are reversed.","Neither child is learning by watching a model.","The sticker is a consequence of behaviour, which is operant.","Automatic crying to a stimulus is an involuntary, classical response."],"k":"Operant = voluntary + consequences; classical = involuntary + pairing.","rn":[["","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical: stimulus with response; Operant: behaviour with consequences"],["","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical vs operant conditioning: type of behaviour","Classical: reflex (unlearned) response; Operant: voluntary behaviour"]]},{"id":8130,"t":"Classical Conditioning","s":"A 50-year-old man with chronic pain has taken the same white tablet for years, and it always relieves his pain. In a study, he is given an identical-looking white tablet with no active drug, and he reports pain relief. A tutor explains this using one model of learning.<br><br>Which model, and what is the white tablet's role?","o":["Classical conditioning; the tablet's look has become a CS","Operant conditioning; the tablet is negative punishment","Observational learning; the tablet is a role-model","Classical conditioning; the inert tablet is an unconditioned stimulus","Operant conditioning; the pain relief is positive punishment, as it adds something to his experience"],"e":"Step 1: the appearance of the tablet has been repeatedly paired with the active drug (the unconditioned stimulus) that relieves pain (the unconditioned response). Step 2: so the tablet's look has become a conditioned stimulus, and pain relief from the inert tablet is a conditioned response, the placebo effect discussed in class as an example of classical conditioning.","w":["Pain relief is not a punishment.","A tablet is not a model whose behaviour is copied.","An inert tablet only works through learned association, so it is a CS.","Punishment makes behaviour less likely; relief is a conditioned response here."],"k":"Placebo can be explained by classical conditioning.","rn":[["","Which other medical effect may be classical conditioning?","Which other medical effect may be classical conditioning?","The placebo effect"],["","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical vs operant conditioning: what is associated","Classical: stimulus with response; Operant: behaviour with consequences"],["","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Why might the placebo effect be classical conditioning?","Conditioned to feel better; Just by taking a pill"]]}]);
